          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "qa": [
      {
        "tag": "Where to?",
        "html": "Something moves from one place to another. Use a movement preposition: <em>She walks <strong>into</strong> the room. The cat jumps <strong>onto</strong> the sofa. We go <strong>to</strong> the station.</em>"
      },
      {
        "tag": "Where?",
        "html": "Something is already in a place. Use a place preposition: <em>She is <strong>in</strong> the room. The cat is <strong>on</strong> the sofa. We are <strong>at</strong> the station.</em>"
      },
      {
        "tag": "Pairs",
        "html": "<strong>in → into</strong> · <strong>on → onto</strong> · <strong>at → to</strong>. And the opposites: <strong>into ↔ out of</strong> · <strong>onto ↔ off</strong> · <strong>up ↔ down</strong> · <strong>to ↔ from</strong>. For a bus or a train, say <em>get on / get off</em>. For a car or a taxi, say <em>get into / get out of</em>."
      },
      {
        "tag": "Along the way",
        "html": "<strong>across</strong>: from one side to the other (<em>across the road</em>). <strong>through</strong>: in at one end, out at the other (<em>through a tunnel</em>). <strong>along</strong>: following a line (<em>along the river</em>). <strong>past</strong>: going by, not stopping (<em>past the bank</em>). <strong>over</strong>: above, to the other side (<em>over the wall</em>). <strong>round</strong>: in a circle (<em>round the tree</em>). <strong>towards</strong>: in the direction of (<em>towards the door</em>)."
      },
      {
        "tag": "Typical mistakes",
        "html": "✗ <em>I go in the kitchen.</em> → ✓ <em>I go into the kitchen.</em> · ✗ <em>I go to home.</em> → ✓ <em>I go home.</em> · ✗ <em>The train goes across the tunnel.</em> → ✓ <em>The train goes through the tunnel.</em>"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Use <em>to</em> for the place you are going to: <em>go to school, go to the shop, go to London</em>. Before <em>home</em>, use no preposition: <em>go home, come home</em>. For where something is, see <a href=\"/prepositions-of-place\">Prepositions of place</a>. For directions with commands (<em>Go along this road</em>), see <a href=\"/imperatives\">Imperatives</a>.",
